    "map": "card",
    "id": "d_s5",
    "kind": "step",
    "color": "5",
    "md": "## 5 · Plan: zero-shot attribute mining, Slack first (16 Nov)\n**Why Slack first:** 55.8% of the corpus (285,605 threads) and the least structured source: the title is only the channel, each thread is one vector, and 6 of its 13 semantic questions fail. The other sources have more structure (ticket fields, email headers, CRM records) and are smaller, so a tactic that works on Slack should carry over. *Fight the toughest soldier first, learn how to defeat the whole army.*\n**Papers:** ready schema-driven extractors work zero-shot: GLiNER2 (205M encoder, CPU, entities + classification + structure, Apache 2.0) and NuExtract (0.5–3.8B, JSON templates). Fine-tuned small models still beat zero-shot on extraction (Sub-Billion Super-Frontier: 0.83 vs 0.69 F1; ELMTEX).\nDecision models (Jev, Gemini Decisions, Clef) fit closed-set attributes, but bill per question: ~$35–80 per attribute on 512k."
   }
  },
