   "text": "Why Slack first: 55.8% of the corpus (285,605\nthreads) and the least structured source: the title is\nonly the channel, each thread is one vector, and 6\nof its 13 semantic questions fail. The other sources\nhave more structure (ticket fields, email headers,\nCRM records) and are smaller, so a tactic that\nworks on Slack should carry over. Fight the\ntoughest soldier first, learn how to defeat the whole\narmy.\nPapers: ready schema-driven extractors work\nzero-shot: GLiNER2 (205M encoder, CPU, entities\n+ classification + structure, Apache 2.0) and\nNuExtract (0.5–3.8B, JSON templates). Fine-tuned\nsmall models still beat zero-shot on extraction\n(Sub-Billion Super-Frontier: 0.83 vs 0.69 F1;\nELMTEX).\nDecision models (Jev, Gemini Decisions, Clef) fit\nclosed-set attributes, but bill per question: ~$35–80\nper attribute on 512k.",
   "originalText": "Why Slack first: 55.8% of the corpus (285,605\nthreads) and the least structured source: the title is\nonly the channel, each thread is one vector, and 6\nof its 13 semantic questions fail. The other sources\nhave more structure (ticket fields, email headers,\nCRM records) and are smaller, so a tactic that\nworks on Slack should carry over. Fight the\ntoughest soldier first, learn how to defeat the whole\narmy.\nPapers: ready schema-driven extractors work\nzero-shot: GLiNER2 (205M encoder, CPU, entities\n+ classification + structure, Apache 2.0) and\nNuExtract (0.5–3.8B, JSON templates). Fine-tuned\nsmall models still beat zero-shot on extraction\n(Sub-Billion Super-Frontier: 0.83 vs 0.69 F1;\nELMTEX).\nDecision models (Jev, Gemini Decisions, Clef) fit\nclosed-set attributes, but bill per question: ~$35–80\nper attribute on 512k.",
   "fontSize": 20,
   "fontFamily": 2,
