   "updated": 1795607209664,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "customData": {
    "map": "card",
    "id": "d_s5a",
    "kind": "step",
    "color": "5",
    "md": "## 5a · Reading: GLiNER2 in practice (16 Nov)\n**Idea:** check what GLiNER2 can do before we write the Slack schema.\n**How it works:** [GLiNER2 architecture](obsidian://open?vault=Pajari%20vault&file=DS%20Knowledge%20base%2FLLM%2FGLiNER2%20architecture) in the DS knowledge base (diagram, step by step, models).\n**Sources:** [TDS: GliNER2, extracting structured information from text](https://towardsdatascience.com/gliner2-extracting-structured-information-from-text/) (T. Bratanic, Neo4j, 6 Jan 2026; PDF in ~/Documents), [GitHub fastino-ai/GLiNER2](https://github.com/fastino-ai/GLiNER2), [paper 2507.18546](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18546).\n**The test:** `gliner2-large-v1` (340M) on one 322-token Wikipedia paragraph about Ada Lovelace, into Neo4j. A \"vibe check\", not a benchmark.\n**What it offers:**\n- ✓ One schema, one call: entities + relations + classification + structured records (`create_schema()…extract()`). Fits our \"no second pass on 500k\".\n- ✓ Label descriptions steer it (\"Event: historical events, wars, or conflicts\").\n- ✓ `extract_json`: fields as `name::type::description`, type `str` or `list`; runs on CPU.\n- ✓ (repo) confidence per item, choices for closed fields, LoRA adapters of 2–10 MB, Apache 2.0.\n**What went wrong:**\n- ✗ No inference: `gender` null for everyone, though Ada is called \"daughter\".\n- ✗ Records mix people up: in `extract_json`, Babbage and William King became Ada's parents, Lord Byron got \"reformer\" (that's Anne Isabella). The same `parent_of` as a **relation** was right.\n- ✗ Label names matter: `alias` always filled, `same_as` never, with the same description.\n- ✗ A `description` field gives surface phrases, not summaries.\n- ✗ Every field is optional: `name` can be null, so records need validating (Pydantic).\n- Tasks in one call run in parallel: entities don't constrain relations; relations can't restrict head/tail types.\n**What we keep:**\n- only for what the text states (customer, region, dates, ids, people);\n- one value per field rather than records of many linked fields; links as relations;\n- try 2–3 label names per field; validate every record;\n- inferred attributes (industry, size, purpose) go to classification, checked hardest on the ~200 reference labels;\n- long threads: split by message (the long-text helper chunks at 384 tokens)."
   }
  },
