   "text": "Why: a cheaper reranker has to keep most of what\ntoday's one adds. Measure that once, then build a\nset any alternative can be scored on offline.\n• Benchmark check: 125 semantic questions, the\n  same 50 fused candidates with and without the\n  reranker (free, local)\n• Dev reranking set: 380 semantic questions (dev\n  180 + dev_slack 200, no train), the candidates\n  today's first search (hybrid-ft-union) hands the\n  reranker, scored by gte-reranker-modernbert at\n  1,024 and 8,192 tokens\n• The reranker reads 96.8 documents per question\n  (median 99, 71–100; gte and BM25 share only ~3,\n  so the union is almost both top 50s): 36,798 pairs,\n  each up to 1,024 tokens (61% get cut)\n• Files: exploration/reranker_dev/ (build.py,\n  data/summary.json; candidates and texts\n  gitignored)",
   "originalText": "Why: a cheaper reranker has to keep most of what\ntoday's one adds. Measure that once, then build a\nset any alternative can be scored on offline.\n• Benchmark check: 125 semantic questions, the\n  same 50 fused candidates with and without the\n  reranker (free, local)\n• Dev reranking set: 380 semantic questions (dev\n  180 + dev_slack 200, no train), the candidates\n  today's first search (hybrid-ft-union) hands the\n  reranker, scored by gte-reranker-modernbert at\n  1,024 and 8,192 tokens\n• The reranker reads 96.8 documents per question\n  (median 99, 71–100; gte and BM25 share only ~3,\n  so the union is almost both top 50s): 36,798 pairs,\n  each up to 1,024 tokens (61% get cut)\n• Files: exploration/reranker_dev/ (build.py,\n  data/summary.json; candidates and texts\n  gitignored)",
