   ],
   "updated": 1795607209730,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "customData": {
    "map": "card",
    "id": "rp3_next",
    "kind": "step",
    "color": "5",
    "md": "### Result (25 Nov): letting LateOn look wider\n**Idea:** LateOn is cheap enough to rerank far more candidates than the cross-encoder, so ask gte and BM25 for more than their top 50 each and let LateOn sort them. All 380 dev questions, end to end; ans@5 = the top 5 holds a document that answers the question (Gemini labels, $1.46 for the new pairs).\n\n| Per search | cov. | total@5 | ans@1 | ans@5 |\n|---|--:|--:|--:|--:|\n| CE today, 50 | 51.3% | 40.8% | 63.4% | 83.7% |\n| LateOn, 50 | 51.6% | 36.8% | 58.7% | 80.8% |\n| LateOn, 100 | 60.0% | 38.7% | 59.2% | 82.1% |\n| LateOn, 150 | 65.5% | 38.9% | 58.7% | 81.6% |\n| LateOn, 200 | 68.7% | 38.4% | 58.4% | 80.5% |\n\n- ✓ Top 100 per search is the best depth: ans@5 +1.3 points (about 5 questions, within noise), still 1.6 behind the cross-encoder\n- ✗ Deeper than 100 gets worse: coverage grows, but LateOn lets more wrong documents into the top 5; ans@1 doesn't move at any depth\n- ✗ Whole documents (8,192 tokens) are worse at every depth (total@5 33.9% at 50 vs 36.8% at 1,024): long documents get more chances to match, and LateOn was trained on short ones\n- Cost: about 47 ms per question at ~380 candidates vs 14 ms at ~97"
   }
  },
  {
